IRREVERSIBLE SEQUENCES: handing the prompt to Claude CLI starts one opaque
producer invocation. That process can make zero or more charged remote
interactions because an agent may take multiple turns/use tools and the CLI or
provider may retry. The wrapper performs all local validation and prompt
construction before starting the process. Claude CLI offers no aggregate
idempotency key or durable pre-send protocol, so neither provider calls nor the
logical stage have an exactly-once guarantee.

CRASH-PREFIX RESIDUALS:

| Crash prefix | Durable/local residual | External effect | Retry effect | Observable evidence | Completion |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| before prompt construction completes | partial scratch prompt and possibly an empty sibling stdout temp, removed on normal exit only | none | safe; no charge | scratch files until scratch cleanup | incomplete |
| prompt complete, before Claude accepts stdin | complete scratch prompt plus empty sibling stdout temp | none | safe; no charge | scratch files | incomplete |
| after CLI process start, before it exits | prompt plus possible partial temp stdout | zero or more remote interactions may execute and charge | a new CLI invocation can repeat some or all remote work and charges | provider/CLI diagnostics; no stage artifact | incomplete |
| Claude exits 0, before validation/rename | prompt plus complete temp stdout | opaque remote interaction set completed and may be charged | crash retry starts a new opaque invocation | complete temp file in scratch | incomplete |
| after atomic rename, before engine capture | prompt plus renamed scratch output; after host power loss the rename is not fsync-durable, so output may be absent but is never treated as a captured artifact | opaque remote interaction set completed and may be charged | engine/operator retry starts a new opaque invocation | output may exist locally but run ref does not record it | wrapper complete; stage incomplete |
| after engine captures output | run-ref artifact plus normal scratch residuals until cleanup | opaque remote interaction set completed and may be charged | no retry required; an explicit rerun is a new opaque invocation | run manifest and artifact | complete |

Normal non-crash failures are not retried inside the wrapper. A trap removes
prompt and unpublished output temporaries on normal shell exit; host/process
crashes can leave them for the engine's scratch cleanup.

GLOBAL INVOCATION SEQUENCE: a complete default workflow is sequential, not one
remote interaction total. Each of the five stage wrappers adds at most one
producer CLI process, and each process owns an opaque, unbounded-by-this-wrapper
set of provider interactions; after each producer artifact the existing gate
engine can invoke the configured reviewer seats (and a fallback only when its
primary fails) before the next producer runs. Thus this change introduces at
most five producer CLI invocations per uninterrupted full run, not a bounded
number of provider requests. Reviewer CLI invocations are pre-existing gate
effects and are likewise opaque. The live plan proof can incur one producer CLI
invocation followed by the two L2 reviewer primaries and configured failure
fallbacks. A crash at any prefix can preserve already-captured earlier
artifacts/gates on the run ref while losing current scratch output; resume may
skip captured work, whereas an explicit fresh run or rerun can repeat prior
opaque remote work and charges. The durable boundary between CLI invocations is
the engine's run-ref capture.

TOPOLOGY: one `ExecRunner` process invokes one wrapper and one Claude CLI per
stage. `ExecRunner.Run` resolves `WorktreeDir`, assigns it to `cmd.Dir`, and its
existing `TestExecRunner_CWD` regression proves relative commands and agent
lookups execute inside that pinned worktree. There is no new concurrent writer;
the detached worktree remains disposable and the stage artifact is committed
to the run ref only by the existing engine after the wrapper succeeds.
Every gate rerun receives a fresh scratch/output namespace: the RERUN branch in
`internal/liverun/gate.go` increments `globalRound` and passes
`stageNN-r<round>` as `ScratchDir` to `runAndCaptureStage`. A fresh process
resume likewise creates a new top-level scratch tree. Therefore an existing
`$ETUDE_OUTPUT_FILE` means contamination within the current attempt, not the
normal prior attempt, and failing before the remote invocation is correct.
`ExecRunner.Run` also constructs every child environment with `PATH` as a base
reserved variable before adding the workflow allowlist; `PATH` is not and must
not be duplicated in `env_allowlist`. The existing environment-isolation tests
pin the base set as `PATH` plus the two ETUDE paths, and the live proof exercises
the wrapper's inner `claude` lookup under that exact environment.

DURABILITY CLASS: non-idempotent opaque remote effect, one Claude CLI process
per wrapper process but zero-or-more provider interactions inside it, with no
exactly-once guarantee across a logical-stage retry. The
wrapper creates the unpublished temporary as a sibling in the exact
`$ETUDE_OUTPUT_FILE` directory, requires it to be non-empty, and renames it
atomically on that filesystem only after Claude exits successfully; retry
semantics remain owned by the existing live engine and gate loop.

COMPLETENESS ENUMERATION: the complete configured stage set is `plan`,
`implement`, `verify`, `docs`, and `review`; each receives an inline command
whose sole stage-specific value is its declared skill/agent name. The complete
wrapper boundary set is missing/invalid agent, missing ETUDE paths, missing
agent definition, zero inputs, ordered multi-input success, Claude failure,
empty or whitespace-only successful Claude output, and successful non-empty
output publication, plus a stale pre-existing output path. The exact
stage-to-agent set is `plan`→`dev-planner`,
`implement`→`dev-executor`, `verify`→`dev-qa`, `docs`→`dev-docs-writer`, and
`review`→`dev-pr-reviewer`; all five matching files exist under the checked-in
`.claude/agents/`. The workflow migration guard pins those five literal pairs,
asserts every real agent file exists, and covers the two required allowlisted
names.

Every configured stage declares at least one input. Every checked-in agent file
also pins its model explicitly (`opus` for plan/verify/review and `sonnet` for
implement/docs), so replacing `--model opus` with `--agent` is deliberate: each
stage now selects the model declared by its own producer agent rather than
forcing one registry-wide reviewer model. Agent instructions that suggest
spawning helpers or inspecting ambient files are intentionally constrained by
the wrapper's empty tool set; an Etude producer derives its artifact from the
declared inputs, not hidden host context.

TRUST BOUNDARY: `.etude/workflow.yaml`, wrapper argv, input role filenames, and
the wrapper's prompt framing are trusted repository/engine-produced control
data. Materialized input contents include prior Claude artifacts and are
untrusted data; the wrapper places each inside explicit begin/end delimiters and
states that delimited content is data, not wrapper instruction. The new Claude
response is likewise untrusted and is published only as the stage artifact.
The OAuth token value is secret process input and must never be printed,
persisted in the prompt, exposed to model-driven tools, or published. The
wrapper disables built-in tools, MCP servers, and slash commands, so the model
can consume only Etude's declared digest-bound inputs rather than ambient host
state. It also scans generated stdout in-process for the exact token value and
rejects a match before publication as defense in depth; the token never becomes
a command argument or scratch file.

## Contract decision

A registry seat's `invoke` is a REVIEWER contract: shared prompt on stdin,
structured verdict envelope in `$ETUDE_OUTPUT_FILE`. A workflow stage runner is
a PRODUCER contract: declared inputs plus stage skill in, arbitrary artifact in
`$ETUDE_OUTPUT_FILE`. Reusing `runner: {name: dev}` crosses those contracts and
is the root defect.

Keep `dev` in the registry for schema/default compatibility, but stop using it
as a stage runner. Replace every workflow stage's named runner with an inline
command `bash .etude/dev-stage-runner.sh <stage-skill>`. `bash` is resolved from
PATH before launch; the relative script argument is then resolved inside the
pinned detached worktree. This also passes the skill identity explicitly
without a new reserved environment variable or shell-side YAML parsing.

## Files and implementation

- Add `.etude/dev-stage-runner.sh`:
  - validate one identifier-like agent argument, the three current ETUDE paths,
    an absent output target, and `.claude/agents/<agent>.md` before invoking
    Claude;
  - collect `ETUDE_INPUTS_DIR/*` in lexical order and fail closed on zero inputs;
  - build a prompt under the engine-owned scratch directory, register cleanup
    before writing it, generate an unpredictable per-invocation delimiter, and
    enclose complete input contents as role-labelled untrusted data;
  - invoke `claude -p --no-session-persistence --tools ""
    --strict-mcp-config --disable-slash-commands --agent <agent>` with the
